# Scope the code graph to code we actually maintain.
#
# Measured 2026-09-29, before this file existed: the graph indexed 28,279 files
# and resolved to 451,307 nodes, of which only 1,268 were project sources
# (src 745 + scripts 206 + tools 66 + tests 251 .py files). `archived/` alone
# contributed 27,489 of those files -- vendored onnxruntime checkouts -- so 96%
# of the corpus was third-party code. The cost was not just disk: the top 25
# community hubs were `BIBL Terms: F`, `BIBL Terms: T`, `onnxruntime_git/
# orttraining/...`, `jinja2/__init__.py`, `@jest/types`, `TEST_F`, `dict`,
# `json`, `tuple` and `string`. Four of the twenty-five were project modules.
# A graph whose hubs are a C++ inference runtime and a bibliography is not a
# map of this system, and the queries worth running against it ("what calls
# this?", "what breaks if I change this?") return vendored code.
#
# Why this file and not .gitignore: graphify already honours .gitignore, and
# .gitignore already lists `output/` and `graphify-out/`. Neither exclusion ever
# took effect, because gitignore does not apply to TRACKED paths -- both trees
# were committed at some point, so `git check-ignore` reports them as not
# ignored no matter what is written in the file. `.graphifyignore` is read in
# addition to .gitignore (graphify/detect.py `_load_ignore_patterns`, which
# reads both names and applies last-match-wins), so it excludes by path
# regardless of tracking. That is the only mechanism here that can do the job.
#
# Syntax is gitignore's. Patterns are anchored with a leading slash so they
# cannot accidentally match a same-named directory deeper in the tree.
#
# Deliberately NOT excluded, because hiding them would hide a known problem:
#   src/SCRIPTS/  byte-identical duplicate of scripts/ (see the forensic
#                §9 open items). Two copies in the graph is the accurate
#                picture; the fix is to delete one, not to stop looking.
#   src/CODEX/    74 real .py files, so the directory stays; only its media
#                 and generated reports are dropped (see the extension list).
#   native/ lib/ packages/ gateway-go/  left in: small enough, and they may hold
#                 real source this project still builds.
#
# The top-level list came from enumerating every directory with its size and
# .py count rather than from guessing, after two passes proved that guessing
# is not good enough here. `archive/` is 1 GB of dev-compare snapshots and is
# NOT `archived/`; the first two passes excluded `archived/` and still carried
# 39,133 graph nodes pointing into a directory that no longer exists on disk,
# because those nodes survived in graph.json rather than in the corpus. A
# missing directory is indistinguishable from an unindexed one unless you
# check the name.
#
# Third pass, and the one that actually found the bulk of it. Counting
# `source_file` occurrences in the built graph rather than listing directories
# again: of 94,197 sampled references, `.agents/skills` contributed 25,067 --
# more than all of `src/CORE` at 15,800, and 27% of the corpus. It is 1,261
# skill-definition files, so the graph was substantially a map of its own
# tooling. `lib/` added vendored vis-9.1.2 and tom-select C sources. Neither is
# visible from a directory listing by size, which is why three passes were
# needed: two from the tree, one from the artifact.
